      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "吗 (ma) is placed at the end of a statement to turn it into a yes/no question."
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "hskId": 1,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "不 (bù) is placed before a verb or adjective to make the sentence negative."
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "hskId": 2,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "过 (guo) is placed after a verb to indicate that someone has experienced something before."
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "hskId": 2,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "正在 (zhèngzài) is placed before a verb to indicate that an action is happening right now."
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "hskId": 2,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "比 (bǐ) is used to compare two people or things."
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "hskId": 5,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "虽然 (suīrán) introduces a concessive clause meaning 'although' and is often followed by 但是 (dànshì)."
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "hskId": 3,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "把 (bǎ) places the object before the verb and emphasizes what happens to that object."
    },
    {
      "id": 8,
      "hskId": 3,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "越来越 (yuèláiyuè) means 'more and more' and describes a gradual change."
    },
    {
      "id": 9,
      "hskId": 3,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "除了 (chúle) can mean 'besides' or 'except for', depending on the sentence structure."
    },
    {
      "id": 10,
      "hskId": 5,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "既然 (jìrán) introduces a known reason or fact and is commonly followed by 就 (jiù)."
    },
    {
      "id": 11,
      "hskId": 4,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "即使 (jíshǐ) means 'even if' and introduces a hypothetical condition."
    },
    {
      "id": 12,
      "hskId": 5,
      "lessonId": null,
      "textId": null,
      "sentenceId": null,
      "wordId": null,
      "content": "不仅 (bùjǐn) means 'not only' and is often paired with 而且 (érqiě)."
    }
